    "faq": {
      "type": "state-seo-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "faq_1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who builds decks in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>In New York, you can hire a local deck contractor, build from raw lumber yourself, or use an A-DIY engineered deck kit. A-DIY delivers a complete deck system to your door, and you, a friend or a local handyman can assemble it in a few hours with just a drill.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does it cost to build a deck in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>Contractor prices in New York depend on your region, deck size, materials, height and local labor rates, and quotes in the NYC metro area are usually the highest. A-DIY deck kits start at $3,699 for pressure-treated wood and $5,700 for composite, typically 30–50% less than hiring a contractor. Use our deck cost calculator to compare prices in your area.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is there a cheaper alternative to hiring a deck builder in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. An engineered deck kit gives you a real, structural deck without paying for a contractor’s design time and full labor. A-DIY kits arrive with everything you need, footings included, so there is no cutting, measuring or sourcing of materials.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is A-DIY a New York deck contractor?",
            "answer": "<p>No. A-DIY does not install decks. We design, engineer and deliver complete deck kits anywhere in New York State. You can build it yourself or hire any local handyman, carpenter or contractor to assemble it for you.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I hire someone to assemble an A-DIY deck?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Many New York homeowners hire a local handyman to assemble their A-DIY kit. Because the parts arrive ready to assemble with step-by-step instructions, assembly takes far less time than a custom build, which helps keep labor costs low.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a permit to build a deck in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>Often, yes. It depends on the deck’s size, height, location, whether it is attached to the house and your local rules. The New York State Uniform Code applies throughout the state except New York City, and your city, town or village enforces it. Always check with your local building department before you build.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_7": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the deck permit rules in New York City?",
            "answer": "<p>New York City has its own construction code and permit system, administered by the Department of Buildings. The city may require approved plans prepared by a New York State licensed professional engineer or registered architect, so contact the Department of Buildings before you order or build.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_8": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How deep do deck footings need to be in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>Under the Residential Code of New York State, footings for decks attached to a house must be protected from frost, which usually means extending below the local frost line set by your building department. Freestanding decks are exempt from the frost-line requirement, but some municipalities add their own rules, so always confirm locally.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_9": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I build a deck on uneven or sloped ground in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. An A-DIY deck can be installed over sloped or uneven ground by adjusting the supporting post heights to create a level deck surface. Supported configurations can be elevated to approximately 40 inches using the appropriate posts, foundations and bracing.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_10": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which footings come with an A-DIY deck?",
            "answer": "<p>Every A-DIY kit includes Tuff Block footings. Depending on your property, deck height and local requirements, other foundation systems may be used instead, such as sonotubes, helical piles, ground screws, in-ground posts or approved surface footing systems.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_11": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is pressure-treated wood or composite better for New York weather?",
            "answer": "<p>Both work well in New York. Pressure-treated wood costs less upfront and can be stained or sealed, but it needs regular upkeep through snow, freeze-thaw cycles and humid summers. Composite costs more but needs no sanding, staining or sealing, resists fading and splintering, and its boards carry a 25-year warranty.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_12": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How strong is an A-DIY deck?",
            "answer": "<p>Applicable A-DIY deck configurations are engineered to hold up to 15,000 pounds when properly assembled, supported and installed according to the applicable specifications. Every A-DIY deck system also includes a five-year structural warranty.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_13": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long does delivery to New York take?",
            "answer": "<p>A-DIY delivers to cities and towns across New York State, typically within 10–14 business days. Your deck arrives with all components and hardware included, so you can start building as soon as it arrives.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_14": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I finance a deck in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. You can choose Build Now, Pay Later financing through Affirm (subject to approval), 0% split payments over up to eight months, or A-DIY Reserve, a $250 deposit that locks in today’s price for a future delivery date.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_15": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can A-DIY engineering diagrams help with my permit?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Diagrams for an applicable A-DIY configuration show dimensions, structural layout, beam, joist and post locations, lumber sizes and major connections, which can support your permit application. Your building department may still request a survey, site plan or stamped documents, and approval is not guaranteed.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
